Visual-layout implementation checklist

The normal page order remains `layout-default`. The two diagnostic orders are
selected explicitly through the command actions `layout-container` and
`layout-rectangle`; neither joins the ordinary page-view cycle.

- [x] Define the layout-order contract and fixture cases: stable fallback,
      rectangular row ordering, container-aware reordering, RTL, overlap,
      zero-size content, tables, popups, frames, and current-element restoration.
- [x] Collect opt-in visual geometry from AX and PAGE extraction in both browser
      engines without adding geometry work to `layout-default` snapshots.
- [x] Implement and conformance-test `layout-rectangle`: order semantic blocks by
      rendered rows and inline position, preserve stable DOM order for ties and
      unplaced blocks, and keep block identity and activation metadata intact.
- [x] Implement and conformance-test `layout-container`: reorder within CSS
      layout containers, preserve coherent content groups, handle flex/grid,
      columns, direction, overlap, sticky/fixed content, and fall back safely.
- [x] Add `layout-default`, `layout-container`, and `layout-rectangle` command
      actions to both readers, including labels, status announcements, rescan,
      live refresh, source switching, and restoration to the same element.
- [ ] Add real-browser fixtures and Firefox/Chromium coverage for a Walmart-like
      purchase card whose DOM position follows reviews but whose visual position
      is beside the product heading; prove all three commands and activation.
- [x] Document the diagnostic layouts, their command-only availability, their
      heuristic limits, and how to return to default order.
- [ ] Run JavaScript, Crystal, focused real-browser, and tmux verification; inspect
      the final diff, commits, and working tree.
